# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# estimai-api environment variables
#
# DATA RESIDENCY — MANDATORY constraints (spec 001, CLAUDE.md):
#   1. EU region: this service MUST be deployed to an EU region only.
#      Production target: Railway EU (europe-west4) — see .railway/railway.ts.
#      No estimate data may be stored outside the EU.
#   2. No body logging: request/response bodies (including estimate `content`
#      JSONB) must NEVER appear in application logs. hono/logger is configured
#      to emit method+path+status only (see src/index.ts). Do NOT add any
#      logger that includes body, query parameters with content, or the Prisma
#      'query' log level.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ─── CORS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated list of allowed origins (no wildcards).
# Must include the UI origin(s) for each environment.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:3000

# ─── Auth (JWT resource-server verification) ─────────────────────────────────
# auth service JWKS endpoint — used by jwtMiddleware to verify RS256 tokens.
# Both values are required; the service will refuse to start if either is missing.
#
# IMPORTANT: use /auth/jwks (better-auth's built-in JWKS endpoint) NOT
# /.well-known/jwks.json — the latter serves the env-var key (kid:
# operai-auth-rs256-v1) which is a DIFFERENT key from the one better-auth's
# jwt plugin uses (stored in the jwks table, dynamic kid). The /auth/token
# endpoint signs with the DB keypair, so estimai-api must verify against
# /auth/jwks.
AUTH_JWKS_URL=http://localhost:3001/auth/jwks
AUTH_ISSUER=http://localhost:3001

# Suite-wide JWT audience (ADR 20260713-jwt-audience-claim-enforcement). Must exactly match the `audience` claim
# the auth service stamps via its jwt plugin's definePayload hook — a mismatch
# (or a token that predates the claim) is rejected with 401. Treat this as a
# single suite-wide constant, documented identically in auth/.env.example and
# every resource server's .env.example (estimai-api, notify-api), not a
# per-service bespoke value (until per-service audience scoping, see ADR 20260713-jwt-audience-claim-enforcement
# Option B follow-up).
AUTH_AUDIENCE=operai-suite

# ─── Size guard ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Added by T5 — per-estimate content size cap. Default: 1048576 (1 MiB).
# MAX_ESTIMATE_BYTES=1048576
#
# Added by OWASP fix — bulk-import raw body limit. If unset, defaults to:
#   min(MAX_ESTIMATE_BYTES × 200 + 64 KiB, 32 MiB) = 32 MiB (for the 1 MiB default).
# This gives each of the 200 elements room for the per-element 1 MiB cap plus a
# small envelope. The 32 MiB absolute ceiling is the DoS trade-off: the service is
# internal/behind-auth so a generous-but-bounded ceiling is acceptable.
# Override only if you intentionally raise MAX_ESTIMATE_BYTES.
# MAX_IMPORT_REQUEST_BYTES=33554432

# ─── Estimate sharing: outbound `auth` clients (specs/20260807-estimate-sharing, ──
# T5, T13) — ENFORCED, REQUIRED ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Base URL of the `auth` service, used ONLY for two Bearer-FORWARDED (the
# caller's own JWT, forwarded as-is — never a service credential) calls:
# `POST /authz/app-access-check` (eligibility DECISION for a prospective
# collaborator — fails CLOSED on error, surfaced to the caller as 503,
# ADR 20260807-third-party-app-access-decision-endpoint-forwarded-jwt; this is the one exception to ADR 20260807-record-level-acl-resource-server-owned-access-primitive's "estimai-api does NOT
# become an authorization-enforcing resource server") and
# `POST /authz/users/identities` (owner/collaborator display-identity
# resolution — fails SOFT to `status:"unknown"`, ADR 20260807-live-identity-resolution-by-id-fail-soft). Distinct from
# AUTH_ISSUER/AUTH_JWKS_URL above (JWT claim-verification values, not
# necessarily a dereferenceable HTTP base) — mirrors refund-api's own
# AUTH_BASE_URL (refund-api/.env.example).
AUTH_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001

# ─── notify-api internal call (specs/20261013-notify-service-credentials, ADR 20261013-all-notify-callers-scoped-credentials-shared-token-retired) — ENFORCED, REQUIRED ─────
#
# Collaborator grant/removal push (POST /system/notifications) authenticates
# with estimai-api's OWN scoped service credential, not a shared token: on
# demand it exchanges these two values at `auth POST /system/service-token`
# (AUTH_BASE_URL above) for a 5-minute JWT with scope `notifications.push`.
# The push fires AFTER the DB transaction commits and NEVER throws — if `auth`
# or notify-api is down only the notification is dropped (ADR 20260807-estimai-api-third-notify-internal-token-holder point 2).
#
# SERVICE_CLIENT_ID must be a key of auth's SERVICE_CLIENTS, and
# SERVICE_CLIENT_SECRET (min 32 characters) must equal that entry's `secret`
# byte for byte — otherwise every push fails with a 401 that only shows up as
# a `[notify]` log line. Locally generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32` and
# paste the same value into auth/.env's SERVICE_CLIENTS. Never logged; never
# commit a real value. The service refuses to start if either is missing.
SERVICE_CLIENT_ID=estimai-api
SERVICE_CLIENT_SECRET=change-me-at-least-32-characters-same-as-in-auth-SERVICE_CLIENTS

# notify-api's base URL for this internal call. Local: notify-api's own dev
# port (8081). Production (Railway): notify-api's PRIVATE-networking address,
# written as `http://${{notify-api.RAILWAY_PRIVATE_DOMAIN}}:${{notify-api.PORT}}`
# (NOT a hand-typed `notify-api.railway.internal` — see infra/README.md's ⚠
# box on `RAILWAY_PRIVATE_DOMAIN`), NOT its public domain — same posture as
# auth's/refund-api's own NOTIFY_INTERNAL_URL.
NOTIFY_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:8081

# ─── crm-api estimate notices (close/reopen push) — OPTIONAL ────────────────
# crm-api's base URL for `POST /system/estimate-notices`, called after an
# estimate is closed or reopened, with the same service JWT (scope
# `estimate-notices.send`, granted to estimai-api in auth's SERVICE_CLIENTS).
# Unset → notices are disabled (one startup log line); the close/reopen
# itself still works. Production: crm-api's PRIVATE-networking address,
# `http://${{crm-api.RAILWAY_PRIVATE_DOMAIN}}:${{crm-api.PORT}}`.
CRM_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:8084

# ─── Third-party lookup timing/rate-limit knobs (specs/20260807-estimate-sharing, ─
# AC-1.2, plan.md "The third-party lookup") — ENFORCED, REQUIRED ───────────
#
# Response-time floor applied to the WHOLE generic-rejection response of
# `POST /estimates/{id}/collaborators` — covers both AC-1.2 causes
# (ineligible target, self-share) plus the round trip to `auth`'s own
# APP_ACCESS_CHECK_FLOOR_MS floor, so the two rejection causes stay
# indistinguishable by timing. Default 300ms; the success path is NOT
# floored.
SHARE_LOOKUP_FLOOR_MS=300

# In-process sliding-window rate limit for `POST /estimates/{id}/collaborators`,
# counted on EVERY attempt (success, duplicate, self, and generic rejection
# alike) — default 20 attempts / 10 minutes per caller `sub`. Same reusable
# rateLimiter.ts interface as auth's own (plan.md Risk R7 — a shared store is
# a later swap, not a rewrite, if this service is ever horizontally scaled;
# see infra/README.md's single-instance note for why it must NOT be scaled
# today).
SHARE_ADD_RATE_LIMIT=20
SHARE_ADD_RATE_WINDOW_MS=600000

# ─── Server ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=8080
NODE_ENV=development

# specs/20261103-projects-handoff (ADR 20261104-estimai-api-first-system-route-estimate-man-days-for-a-service-credential): audience of the service credentials /system/* accepts; equal to auth's SERVICE_AUDIENCE
SERVICE_AUDIENCE=operai-service
